        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "e9d72765-a9b7-572e-8028-3699a565b429",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Jackson Davis, Francis Key Hunt, Francis Scott Key\n- Places: Loudoun House, Terra Rubra, Keysville, Maryland, Frederick, Maryland, Georgetown, D.C., Washington, D.C.\n- Topics: architecture, birth sites, National Register of Historic Places, United States national anthem\n\nFacts:\n- Francis Scott Key was born at Terra Rubra in 1779.\n- Francis Scott Key was an American lawyer, author, and amateur poet from Frederick, Maryland, and later Georgetown, D.C., near Washington, D.C.\n- Francis Scott Key wrote the lyrics to a poem first titled \"The Defence of Fort McHenry,\" which later became the United States national anthem, \"The Star-Spangled Banner,\" when set to an old English gentlemen's society tune.\n- Loudoun House was designed by New York architect Alexander Jackson Davis and built in 1851 for Francis Key Hunt.\n- Francis Key Hunt lived from 1817 to 1879 and was named after his mother's cousin, Francis Scott Key.\n- Terra Rubra was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1978.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
